  },
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A straight wire AB of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> and length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> is suspended by a pair of flexible leads in uniform magnetic field of magnitude <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math> as shown in the figure. The magnitude of the current required in the wire to remove the tension in the supporting leads is _____ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>. (Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced open=\"\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math>.</p><p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"assets\\qt0063_a62d655c79de.png\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 181px;\" /></p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "2",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The formula to calculate the magnetic force on the conductor is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>The formula to calculate the gravitational force on the conductor is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>Equate both the equations and simplify to obtain the required current through the conductor.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right center left\" columnspacing=\"0px\"><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>&#8658;</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>3</mn></mfenced></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p><p>Substitute the values of the known parameters into equation (3) to calculate the required current through the conductor.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right center left\" columnspacing=\"0px\"><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><mn>40</mn><mn>1000</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p></blockquote>"
  }
}